---
// Case study. Every number on this page must come verbatim from the vendor-vetting README or
// eval-results/2026-08-19-0251-qwen2.5-7b.md. Never call the project "agentic": it's a staged pipeline.
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Button from "@/components/Button.astro";
import GitHubIcon from "@/components/icons/GitHub.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/CarlosJ09/vendor-vetting";
const DEMO = "https://vendor-vetting.vercel.app/";
const RESULTS = `${REPO}/blob/main/eval-results/2026-08-19-0251-qwen2.5-7b.md`;

const TLDR = [
  "<strong>The model extracts. The code validates.</strong> A test fails the build if decision words appear in a prompt.",
  "<strong>0 of 16</strong> defective dossiers were reported as <code>pass</code>, the one metric that matters.",
  "Self-reported confidence was <strong>1.00 on all 761 fields</strong>, including the one wrong extraction. It carried no information.",
  "The only extraction error looked like a model failure. It was <strong>my own text-reconstruction bug</strong>.",
  "Runs fully local on an <strong>8 GB laptop GPU</strong>. The demo is Next.js on Vercel + Supabase, with TLS that fails closed.",
];

const STAGES = [
  { name: "ingest", note: "Magic bytes, not filenames. SHA-256 and duplicate detection." },
  { name: "render", note: "PDFs become a text layer plus per-fragment geometry. Images stay images: no deskew, no denoise." },
  { name: "classify", model: true, note: "One of 4 document types, or <code>unknown</code>." },
  { name: "extract", model: true, note: "Per-type schema, structured output: <code>{ value, confidence, page }</code>." },
  { name: "normalize", note: "Dates parsed, identifiers stripped. Deterministic." },
  { name: "locate", note: "Find each value in the text layer and record its bounding box." },
  { divider: true },
  { name: "validate", note: "Check digits, date sanity, freshness, status." },
  { name: "cross-check", note: "RNC, razón social and representante legal agree across documents." },
  { name: "dossier", note: "<code>pass</code> · <code>warn</code> · <code>fail</code> · <code>needs_review</code> per requirement, each linked to its evidence." },
];

const RESULTS_ROWS = [
  { metric: "Dossier verdicts", value: "40 / 40", detail: "100.0%" },
  { metric: "Classification", value: "160 / 160", detail: "including 40/40 degraded cédula scans" },
  { metric: "Extraction", value: "100%", detail: "precision and recall on all 19 fields" },
  { metric: "Latency per dossier", value: "36.29s", detail: "p50 · p95 50.02s" },
  { metric: "Tokens per dossier", value: "10,586", detail: "395,003 in · 28,422 out, whole corpus" },
  { metric: "Measured cost", value: "$0.00", detail: "local inference" },
];
---

<Layout
  title="vendor-vetting: case study · Carlos Jiménez"
  description="An LLM pipeline that vets supplier documents against the Dominican Republic's procurement registry requirements. The model extracts; tested code decides. Results, architecture and what went wrong."
>
  <main class="flex-1">
    <article class="cs mx-auto w-full max-w-page px-5 sm:px-8 pt-12 sm:pt-20">
      <a href="/#projects" class="font-mono text-xs text-muted hover:text-accent">← All projects</a>

      <header class="mt-10">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">
          Case study · Personal project
        </p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 text-5xl sm:text-6xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.05]">
          vendor-vetting
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-5 text-2xl leading-snug text-muted text-pretty">
          An LLM pipeline that vets supplier documents for the Dominican Republic's public
          procurement registry. The model reads; tested code decides.
        </p>

        <dl class="mt-8 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-2 font-mono text-xs sm:grid-cols-[6rem_1fr]">
          <dt class="text-muted">Stack</dt>
          <dd>Next.js · TypeScript · PostgreSQL + Drizzle · Vitest · Ollama</dd>
          <dt class="text-muted">Models</dt>
          <dd>qwen2.5:7b (text) + qwen2.5vl:7b (vision), Q4_K_M</dd>
          <dt class="text-muted">Status</dt>
          <dd>Deployed demo · open source</dd>
        </dl>

        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <Button href={DEMO} variant="primary" external>Live demo</Button>
          <Button href={REPO} external>
            <GitHubIcon class="size-4" />
            Code
          </Button>
        </div>
      </header>

      <aside class="mt-14 border-y border-rule py-6" aria-labelledby="tldr">
        <h2 id="tldr" class="!mt-0 font-mono !text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-muted !font-normal">
          In 60 seconds
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2.5">
          {TLDR.map((item) => <li class="dash" set:html={item} />)}
        </ul>
      </aside>

      <p class="mt-6 font-mono text-xs text-muted text-pretty">
        Independent project. No affiliation with the DGCP, the DGII, the TSS, or any Dominican state
        institution. Every document in it is synthetic.
      </p>

      <!-- 1 -->
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        To register as a state supplier in the Dominican Republic, a company submits a folder of
        certifications: proof it legally exists, proof it is current on its taxes, proof it is
        current on social security, and the ID of whoever signs on its behalf. Today a person opens
        each file, reads the dates, checks the identifiers, and compares the company name across all
        four.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is careful, repetitive work, and the failure that matters is not rejecting a good packet.
        It is <em>accepting a bad one</em>, because nobody looks again.
      </p>
      <p>
        vendor-vetting takes an unlabeled folder, works out what each file is, extracts the fields,
        validates them, cross-checks the documents against each other, and produces a dossier with a
        status per requirement, each linked to the exact page and region that supports it.
      </p>

      <!-- 2 -->
      <h2>The one principle</h2>
      <blockquote class="pull">The model extracts. The code validates.</blockquote>
      <p>
        The model's only job is to read a document and report what it says, with a confidence score
        and a page citation. It is never asked to judge, decide, or reason about a business rule.
      </p>
      <div class="grid gap-px bg-rule border border-rule my-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="bg-paper p-5">
          <p class="!m-0 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-accent">Asked of the model</p>
          <p class="!mt-2 !mb-0 italic">"What issue date appears on this document?"</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-paper p-5">
          <p class="!m-0 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted">Never asked</p>
          <p class="!mt-2 !mb-0 italic">"Is this document expired?"</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Expiry, check digits, status thresholds and cross-document consistency are deterministic
        code with unit tests. A date arithmetic bug is findable and fixable; a model that decided
        "expired" for reasons it cannot show you is neither.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is enforced, not just intended: <code>src/models/prompts.test.ts</code> fails the build if
        the words <em>vencido, vigente, válido, aprobar, rechazar, cumple</em>, a day-count window, or
        any cross-document comparison appear in a prompt. It has caught me once already, on a prompt
        where I had written "un acuerdo de pago <strong>vigente</strong>".
      </p>
      <h3>Confidence is a third answer, not a threshold</h3>
      <p>
        A field extracted below 0.75 confidence is <strong>not</strong> rejected. It goes to a review
        queue, and the requirement that depends on it reports <code>needs_review</code>, which outranks
        even <code>fail</code>. An extraction the system doesn't trust can't prove a document is bad
        any more than it can prove it is good. Auto-rejecting would turn "I couldn't read this" into
        "this supplier is non-compliant".
      </p>

      <!-- 3 -->
      <h2>Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        A staged pipeline. Two stages call a model; everything after <code>locate</code> is plain code.
      </p>
      <ol class="pipeline" aria-label="Pipeline stages, in order">
        {
          STAGES.map((stage) =>
            stage.divider ? (
              <li class="divider" aria-label="No model past this line">
                <span>No model past this line</span>
              </li>
            ) : (
              <li>
                <span class="name">
                  {stage.name}
                  {stage.model && <span class="tag">model</span>}
                </span>
                <span class="note" set:html={stage.note} />
              </li>
            ),
          )
        }
      </ol>
      <p>
        Every stage writes its duration, tokens and output to <code>stage_runs</code>, so a stage can
        be re-run without repeating the model calls before it, and so the latency and cost below are
        measured rather than estimated.
      </p>
      <p>
        The model provider is an interface with exactly two methods, <code>classify</code> and
        <code>extract</code>. Nothing above it knows which model answered, which is what makes the
        eval portable across backends.
      </p>
      <h3>Deployed, and TLS fails closed</h3>
      <p>
        All model work happens at ingest time, so the deployed app is stateless: Next.js on Vercel
        reading Supabase Postgres, with no Ollama in production. Supabase signs its certificates with
        a private root that Node doesn't trust, and the fix the internet reaches for first is
        <code>rejectUnauthorized: false</code>. That isn't a smaller version of TLS; it's TLS with the
        part that stops an impostor removed. Here a remote database without a CA certificate fails
        loudly instead of silently downgrading.
      </p>

      <!-- 4 -->
      <h2>Results</h2>
      <p>
        qwen2.5:7b (text) and qwen2.5vl:7b (vision), both Q4_K_M, via Ollama on a local RTX 4070
        Laptop (8 GB). 40 synthetic dossiers, 160 documents.
      </p>
      <div class="my-10 border-l-2 border-accent pl-6">
        <p class="!m-0 font-mono text-5xl sm:text-6xl tabular-nums text-accent">0 / 16</p>
        <p class="!mt-3 !mb-0 text-pretty">
          defective dossiers reported as <code>pass</code>. A defective dossier reported clean tells a
          reviewer there is nothing to look at, so this is reported on its own and never averaged
          into anything.
        </p>
      </div>
      <table class="results">
        <caption class="sr-only">Eval results</caption>
        <tbody>
          {
            RESULTS_ROWS.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.metric}</th>
                <td>
                  <span class="value">{row.value}</span>
                  <span class="detail">{row.detail}</span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs text-muted">
        Source: <a class="link" href={RESULTS} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >eval-results/2026-08-19-0251-qwen2.5-7b.md ↗</a
        >
      </p>

      <h3>How to read these numbers</h3>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          A 100% on a corpus I generated myself is not evidence the system reads real documents. It
          is evidence of something narrower and still worth having: that the pipeline is correct end
          to end, and that when a verdict is wrong the fault is unambiguously in extraction rather
          than in the rules.
        </p>
        <p>
          Expect these numbers to fall on real documents. The eval harness exists so that when they
          do, the drop is measured instead of discovered by a supplier.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        I predicted the degraded cédula scans, rotated, noisy and partially cropped, would be where
        a 7B quantized vision model lost the most ground. I was wrong: it classified 40/40 and
        extracted all four fields without an error.
      </p>

      <!-- 5 -->
      <h2>What went wrong</h2>

      <h3>Self-reported confidence carried no information</h3>
      <p>
        Across the full corpus, <strong>761 extracted fields, every single one came back at
        confidence exactly 1.00</strong>. Minimum 1.00, maximum 1.00, zero variance. The one
        extraction error the pipeline ever made also came back at 1.00.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the threshold, the review queue and the <code>needs_review</code> status are built,
        tested and wired end to end, and on this model the signal that drives them is a constant. The
        queue shows "nothing pending" not because the extractions were verified, but because the
        model never once expressed a doubt. Asking a model how sure it is returns a number shaped
        like evidence; here it was decoration.
      </p>
      <p>
        The design already assumed this. Low confidence routes to a human, never to rejection, and
        no requirement is decided by confidence alone. That's why the verdicts are still 40/40 with a
        useless confidence signal. The honest alternative, per-token logprobs, isn't usefully exposed
        by the Ollama chat API.
      </p>

      <h3>The model got blamed for my bug</h3>
      <p>
        An extraction returned <code class="!whitespace-normal">"Empleador Servicios Punta Alta, E.I.R.L."</code>: the field label
        glued to the value, which then failed the cross-document name check. It looked like a model
        failure. It was mine. <code>fragmentsToText</code> joined every fragment on a line with a
        single space, so a ~100pt column gutter and a word space became the same character.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fixed by emitting a tab when the horizontal gap exceeds 2% of the page width. It was the only
        extraction error in the corpus. Worth saying plainly: my first instinct was to reach for the
        prompt.
      </p>

      <h3>The synthetic watermark broke classification</h3>
      <p>
        Cédulas first came back as <code>unknown</code>. Asked why, the model pointed at the
        watermark: <em>"EJEMPLAR SINTETICO - SIN VALIDEZ LEGAL"</em>. The marking the project needs
        for honesty was making the model reject the document as not-a-document. An instruction that
        such markings don't determine document type fixed it. The general hazard: safety markings on
        synthetic eval data can become the thing the model keys on.
      </p>

      <h3>Losing the GPU was silent</h3>
      <p>
        Restarting Docker Desktop broke WSL's GPU passthrough, and Ollama didn't error. It fell back
        to CPU and kept answering. The vision model stopped finishing inside the 180-second timeout,
        every scanned cédula failed, and seven dossiers were written as <code>fail</code> for a
        reason that exists nowhere in the documents.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything pointed at the model: timeouts, in the extract stage, only on the vision path. The
        tell was elsewhere: <code>/api/ps</code> reporting the model resident with
        <code>size_vram: 0</code>. A backend that degrades instead of failing will hand you a
        plausible wrong story about your own code. The fix was a check, not a code change: warm the
        vision model and assert it landed in VRAM before a run.
      </p>

      <!-- 6 -->
      <h2>What I'd do next</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        <li class="dash">
          <strong>Measure a hosted model against the same eval.</strong> A model with real logprobs is
          the first thing to compare, because it could give the confidence signal some meaning. The
          provider interface makes this a comparison, not a rewrite.
        </li>
        <li class="dash">
          <strong>Run it on real documents.</strong> The synthetic corpus proves the pipeline, not
          the reading. Real intake brings scanner artifacts, phrasings and layouts I didn't
          anticipate.
        </li>
        <li class="dash">
          <strong>Evidence regions for scans.</strong> Literal PDF fields are located 522 of 522; scanned
          cédulas get no bounding box, and the viewer says so rather than drawing a plausible one.
          Fixing that needs per-word OCR geometry the vision path doesn't produce yet.
        </li>
        <li class="dash">
          <strong>Image preprocessing, only when it earns its place.</strong> No deskew or denoise
          today, on purpose: each would become an untested pipeline whose effect on the numbers
          nobody could separate.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 7 -->
      <h2>Links</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <Button href={DEMO} variant="primary" external>Live demo</Button>
        <Button href={REPO} external>
          <GitHubIcon class="size-4" />
          Code &amp; README
        </Button>
        <Button href={RESULTS} external>Full eval report</Button>
      </div>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs text-muted text-pretty">
        The demo runs on a free tier that sleeps when nobody visits. If it shows no data, it's waking
        up. Everything also reproduces locally with no account anywhere.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
